  <article class="legal-page">
    <h1>How we review 3D printing equipment</h1>

    <p class="lead">
      A short, honest explanation of how the content on FilamentPath is built &mdash;
      what we evaluate, what we don't, and where the limitations are.
    </p>

    <h2>Editorial principles</h2>

    <p>
      Recommendations on this site lean on three sources, in order of weight:
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Manufacturer specifications and datasheets.</strong>
        Build volume, layer height range, max print speed, bed and nozzle
        temperature limits, hotend and extruder design, compatible
        materials, dimensions, weight, warranty. We collect these from
        official product pages and verify across at least two sources
        when something looks inconsistent.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Aggregate user reports.</strong> Long-running threads on
        Reddit (r/3Dprinting, r/BambuLab, r/PrusaSlicer, r/FixMyPrint),
        forum threads on All3DP, and verified-purchase reviews on
        Amazon. Threads with technical specifics and reproducible
        failure modes weigh more than short opinion posts.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Reviewer consensus across independent outlets.</strong>
        YouTube channels and 3D-printing publications that disclose
        whether the unit was purchased or provided by the manufacturer.
        We note when reviews involve manufacturer-loaned units.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What we evaluate</h2>

    <p>
      Each product page covers, where applicable, these dimensions:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Print quality.</strong> Layer consistency, dimensional accuracy, surface finish, bridging and overhang behaviour out of the box.</li>
      <li><strong>Bed adhesion consistency.</strong> Auto-leveling reliability, build surface durability, first-layer behaviour across PLA, PETG, and (when relevant) higher-temperature materials.</li>
      <li><strong>Print speed reliability.</strong> Manufacturer claims vs realistic throughput at acceptable quality. Vibration compensation, input shaping, and ringing/ghosting at advertised speeds.</li>
      <li><strong>Ease of leveling and setup.</strong> Time from box-open to first usable print, calibration burden, slicer profile availability.</li>
      <li><strong>Parts availability and serviceability.</strong> Hotend, nozzle, build plate, and electronics replacement parts &mdash; how easy and how expensive.</li>
      <li><strong>Value per build volume.</strong> Cost-per-cubic-mm of usable build space, after accounting for must-have accessories that aren't in the box.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What we are transparent about</h2>

    <h3>We don't physically own every product we cover.</h3>
    <p>
      Covering the long tail of FDM printers, filament brands, and
      accessories would require a print farm, not a desk. We label
      content honestly: analyses based on aggregated research are not
      labeled as hands-on tests. When the operator has direct experience
      with a specific printer or filament, that's noted in the body of
      the page.
    </p>

    <h3>We are funded by affiliate commissions.</h3>
    <p>
      Many product links are Amazon affiliate links. As an Amazon
      Associate, we earn from qualifying purchases at no extra cost to
      you. Manufacturers do not pay us for coverage, placement, or
      rankings. We do not accept free units in exchange for favorable
      reviews. Where direct programs exist (Bambu Lab, Creality, Anycubic
      and similar), we'll disclose them by name on each affected page.
    </p>

    <h3>We update content when the underlying facts change.</h3>
    <p>
      Printers get firmware updates that change real-world behaviour.
      Manufacturers tweak hotend designs between revisions. Prices drift
      hard in this market. Pages note their last review date, and we
      revisit popular recommendations at least quarterly to confirm they
      still hold up.
    </p>

    <h2>What we don't do</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>We don't post fake reviews or fabricate first-print anecdotes.</li>
      <li>We don't accept payment for ranking position.</li>
      <li>We don't recommend a printer outside the budget or use case the reader specified &mdash; even when a more expensive option would pay us more.</li>
      <li>We don't pretend a $200 printer matches a $700 one. When the gap is real, we say so. When it doesn't matter for the reader's use case (e.g. printing PLA minis on the kitchen table), we say that too.</li>
      <li>We don't write content optimised only for search rankings at the cost of usefulness. If a comparison adds nothing for the reader, we don't publish it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How to flag an error</h2>

    <p>
      If you notice a specification error, a stale price, or a product
      that no longer exists, email
      <a href="mailto:hello@filamentpath.com">hello@filamentpath.com</a>.
      We correct material errors within 48 hours and update the page's
      last-reviewed date.
    </p>

    <h2>Limitations of this methodology</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>
        Specs don't capture everything. Two printers with the same
        published max speed and identical claimed accuracy can produce
        noticeably different parts; we weight aggregate user reports
        against this gap, but it remains a limitation.
      </li>
      <li>
        New printers enter the market faster than reliable user
        consensus can form. For releases under 6 months old,
        recommendations are explicitly marked as preliminary.
      </li>
      <li>
        Our coverage skews toward Amazon-availability. Direct-from-manufacturer
        options (Prusa Research, Bambu Lab store, Voron community kits)
        are referenced contextually, but only get affiliate links when
        they're also on Amazon.
      </li>
      <li>
        We start FDM-first. Resin (SLA / MSLA) printers carry safety
        and handling considerations &mdash; uncured resin is irritant
        and requires PPE and proper ventilation &mdash; that we'll cover
        explicitly when we expand to that segment, not by inference from
        FDM coverage.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>About the operator</h2>

    <p>
      This site is operated by a single editor &mdash; a Colombian data
      scientist relocated abroad &mdash; who runs an FDM 3D printer at
      home as a maker hobbyist. More background on the
      <a href="/about/">About</a> page.
    </p>
  </article>
